@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: retire the privileged dind sidecar and make sbx the only way an agent gets a daemon
' covers: internal/manifest/**
' covers: internal/run/**
' covers: internal/sbx/agents.go, internal/sbx/sbx_test.go
' covers: cmd/proveo/main.go, cmd/proveo/clean.go
' covers: internal/contract/contract_test.go
' covers: e2e/sbx_test.go, e2e/hello_world_test.go
' session: d2ff9d04-cba3-46b5-87d6-698c921c9ede
' APPLIED 2026 — all four steps landed; kept as the decision record for why
' no privileged sidecar exists. Cited from the source it constrains.
'
' ── 2026-10-16: THE SECOND DECISION THIS FILE DECLINED WAS TAKEN ───────────
' This plan kept docker+egress as the PROVEO_SBX=0 fallback and left mitmproxy
' and Squid out of scope. The Executor then chose "Retire Docker egress",
' "Refuse without sbx" and "Retire review, map the rest". The backend, its
' sidecars, the review tier and the fallback are DELETED
' (_spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml). Every "still stands" and "left
' open" below is therefore HISTORY, answered as marked.
'
' ── 2026-10-17: THE STUBS THIS FILE ARGUED FOR ARE GONE TOO ────────────────
' The user, on the CLI and env stubs: "Drop all four" (--egress-mode, --shell,
' PROVEO_SBX off values, PROVEO_DIND); on the manifest's retired fields: "Drop
' all four" (dind, sandbox_docker, egress, provider). MEASURED: warnDindRetired,
' its PROVEO_DIND warning and test, the PROVEO_SBX off-value refusal, the
' one-day hidden `--egress-mode` refusal, RetiredDind, RetiredSandboxDocker,
' the `docker: dind` special case and TestRetiredDockerDindIsRefused are all
' deleted. This file's own rule — "say so, never silently ignore" — is
' therefore superseded: a stale variable, flag or manifest key now meets the
' generic behaviour (unread, cobra "unknown flag", `invalid docker "dind"
' (want "sbx")`, or an unknown YAML key that loads). Remnants 1 and 4 below
' are HISTORY; remnants 2 and 3 still hold.
' UPDATE 2026-10-17, F10 (MEASURED): the `docker:` key itself is deleted —
' Manifest.Docker, DockerMode and IsSbx are gone, no def declares `docker:`,
' and the decoder runs KnownFields(true), so a stale `docker:`, `dind:`,
' `egress:` or `provider:` key now FAILS loading and names itself
' (TestAStaleManifestKeyFailsLoading). The "loads silently" and `invalid docker`
' outcomes above are HISTORY too; `daemon: false` is the one daemon knob
' (_spec/internal/manifest/harness-manifest-schema.puml).
'
' ── WHAT ACTUALLY LANDED, AND THE TWO THINGS THE PLAN DID NOT KNOW ─────────
' 1. Manifests moved. `opencode` AND `cecli` declare `docker: sbx`; both images
'    grew the layers that makes that true — the whole static docker tarball
'    (dockerd, containerd, runc, docker-proxy, not just the client), `iptables`
'    for the daemon's NAT chain, a `docker` group for the root:docker socket, a
'    real /home/agent, and `com.docker.sandboxes.start-docker`.
' 2. The offer is gone. `docker (dind)` left the execution row, which now carries
'    the one greyed-and-ticked fact every sbx harness already had. `PROVEO_DIND`
'    warned and no-opped rather than being silently unread — until 2026-10-17,
'    when the warning was dropped and the variable became simply unread.
' 3. The lattice is deleted: `internal/dind` whole, its five gates, the
'    interactive prompt, `ScopeHasDockerfiles`, and the dind arm of `gateAddons`.
' 4. The plumbing is deleted: the `--link` args, `DOCKER_HOST`, `WaitReady`,
'    `ConnectNetwork`, and the `--privileged` run. proveo starts no privileged
'    container any more.
'
' THING ONE THE PLAN DID NOT KNOW: sbx's agent list is CLOSED, and cecli is not
' on it. `sbx run --help` prints claude, codex, copilot, cursor, docker-agent,
' droid, gemini, kiro, opencode, shell; a Kit cannot extend it. opencode maps
' straight across. cecli is aider and nothing on that list is aider, so
' `docker: sbx` would have sent it to a backend that could not launch it —
' `sbx.BuiltinAgent("cecli")` returned "" and sbx reads the first workspace path
' as an agent name. `sbx.AgentFor` now resolves an unmapped target to the SHELL
' agent carrying the target's own launch command, which is what makes
' `docker: sbx` mean "runs in a sandbox" for every harness rather than only the
' ones sbx ships an agent for. UNVERIFIED against a real sbx: whether the shell
' agent execs a trailing command is the one claim here nobody measured.
'
' THING TWO: the e2e verdict had to be INVERTED, not moved.
' `assertSandboxReplacesDocker` asserted that a `docker: sbx` sandbox reaches NO
' daemon — true when written, because sbx starts one only for an image carrying
' `com.docker.sandboxes.start-docker` and no proveo image did. With the label a
' daemon answers (Server 29.7.2, measured). Since retiring the sidecar makes
' that daemon the only one a harness gets, a sandbox reaching none is now the
' failure. The boundary half of the old claim moved rather than vanished:
' e2e/sbx_egress_test.go fails when a pull from inside the sandbox reaches a
' registry no Kit allowlists, which is the property the five gates used to buy
' by refusing the daemon outright.
'
' `egress.Plan.AgentNetwork` WENT WITH THE SIDECAR. It recorded the user-defined
' bridge so the sidecar could attach under the alias `docker`, and nothing else
' ever read it. Keeping it was considered and rejected on the question "could a
' network traffic report be produced from it after the run exits?" — it cannot:
' `teardown` emits `network rm` for that network, Docker keeps no per-network
' traffic accounting, this tree captures no packets anywhere, and the ONE posture
' that set the field (open + forward + a local model) is the one whose own posture
' line already reads "none — plain bridge, no MITM, no Squid". Every committed plan
' golden rendered it EMPTY. The topology it described is still asserted, through
' `AgentArgs` and the `--internal` flag on the created network, which is the
' property the old invariant was really protecting.
'
' ── WHAT THE RETIREMENT LEFT BEHIND, AND HOW EACH REMNANT BEHAVES ─────────
' Four places knew the word `dind`, each deliberately. Since 2026-10-17 two do:
' normalizeAddons (2) and `proveo clean`'s `proveo-dind-*` sweep (MEASURED).
'
' 1. HISTORY — THE MANIFEST REFUSED IT BY NAME. `docker: dind` had its own case
'    in Validate, so the error said the privileged sidecar was retired, and two
'    retired KEYS (`dind:`, `sandbox_docker:`) were parsed so a def carrying one
'    could not look honoured. Deleted 2026-10-17 ("Drop all four"): `dind` now
'    gets the generic `invalid docker "dind" (want "sbx")`, and the keys load
'    silently like any unknown key. The only two answers left are one daemon
'    and none.
'
' 2. A CACHED CHOICE IS DROPPED, NOT TRANSLATED. normalizeAddons discards both
'    spellings an older run may have remembered — `dind` and `docker (dind)` —
'    because there is no equivalent offer to carry them to, and the sandbox box
'    is re-ticked by the gate on every run anyway. Translating them would invent
'    consent for a different thing.
'
' 3. THE TIER GATE OUTLIVED THE SIDECAR IT WAS WRITTEN FOR. `gateAddons` lost
'    its dind arm but not its job: it governed the Claude in Chrome bridge on
'    the DOCKER backend, where an intercepting tier left the agent no route to
'    the host. MEASURED 2026-10-16: the tier is gone, and gateAddons greys
'    claude-in-chrome on every sbx run with chromebridge.SbxWhy ("needs a real
'    Claude token — sbx keeps it proxy-managed and the Chrome bridge rejects
'    the sentinel (401)"). chromebridge.TierSupported/TierWhy are deleted.
'
' 4. HISTORY — `PROVEO_DIND` SAID SO RATHER THAN STAYING SILENT. Its whole job
'    was the warning, for an operator who exported it months ago. The warning
'    (warnDindRetired) and its test were deleted 2026-10-17 ("Drop all four");
'    the variable is unread now, and nothing outside _spec/ names it (MEASURED).
'
' ── AND ONE RESERVATION INVERTED ──────────────────────────────────────────
' The contract suite used to assert BOTH directions: a subscription harness must
' set `docker: sbx`, and a non-subscription one must NOT. That second arm
' reserved the sandbox for the two vendor harnesses and left opencode and cecli
' on the privileged sidecar. It is deliberately GONE. sbx is where every harness
' runs; subscription now decides only HOW a credential gets there.
'
' The daemon-coverage guard collapsed the same way. It used to PARTITION two
' branches so neither could report success by testing nothing; with one branch
' left, the guard is the simpler and stronger claim — every def that promises a
' daemon is in the sbx branch, checked against every def rather than a tracked
' subset, and the branch is asserted NON-EMPTY.
'
' ── THE CLIENT INSIDE THE SANDBOX IS NOT proveo'S TO PROMISE ──────────────
' `docker: sbx` promises a DAEMON. Whether a docker CLIENT is on PATH inside the
' sandbox is neither stable nor ours: cursor's sandbox has one and claudecode's
' does not, from images that both install it, because `sbx create` re-bakes the
' template (_spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox.puml) and the claude-flavoured
' bake drops it. Pinning it either way would encode an sbx implementation
' detail as a proveo contract.
'
' It is still NAMED when it bites. assertDockerServerReachable separates
' "command not found" from "cannot connect to the docker daemon", because the
' two need different repairs — the first is sbx-side or a manifest that should
' stop declaring `docker: sbx`, the second is a daemon that did not come up.
' TestDindHarnessesShipADockerClient asserted this for the privileged-sidecar
' branch, where the image alone decided the answer; that branch is retired.
'
' WHAT DID NOT HAPPEN, ON PURPOSE (HISTORY — all of it happened 2026-10-16).
' mitmproxy and Squid stood, the docker backend answered `PROVEO_SBX=0` and
' `--egress-mode review`, and the review tier was still implemented. `clean`
' still sweeps `proveo-dind-*`: a `--privileged` container an older proveo left
' running is the last thing to strand on a host with nothing left that knows
' its name. secret-custody's (d) needed the BACKEND deleted, not the sidecar;
' it closed when the backend went.
'
'   what dind WAS ............... deleted with the package; the record is here
'                                 and in _spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox.puml
'   the backend it moves to ..... _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'   what governs egress there ... _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
'   the tiers this does NOT decide _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
'   the consent flow at stake ... retired 2026-09-16 with the docker review
'                                 gate — _spec/tests/testing-strategy.puml
'   what this UNBLOCKS .......... _spec/_plans/secret-custody.puml
'
' SCOPE. Eliminate the dind sidecar. The fate of mitmproxy and Squid was
' deliberately NOT decided here — they served the docker backend, which
' survived this change as the PROVEO_SBX=0 fallback, and deciding both at once
' would have coupled a security win to an observability regression. Decided
' separately on 2026-10-16: deleted, and the observability answer is "no flow
' record; sbx's policy log is the record".
'
' ── WHAT ELSE WAS WAITING ON THIS (CLOSED 2026-10-16) ──────────────────────
' secret-custody's exposure (d) — a plaintext credential in a mounted file —
' could not be closed while the docker backend survived. There the agent's
' credential IS ~/.claude/.credentials.json in the mounted proveo home, so any
' path that carries a login to the agent writes a plaintext token into a
' bind-mounted directory every later run can read. On sbx that file is written
' INSIDE the sandbox by sbx's own proxy and holds proxy-managed sentinels while
' the real tokens stay in the egress layer.
'
' So (d) closes by REMOVING the backend rather than by patching it, and this
' plan is the removal. Note the tension with the SCOPE above: keeping docker as
' the PROVEO_SBX=0 fallback keeps review, the flow record and Chrome alive AND
' keeps (d) open. That trade is the "one backend, or two?" question below, and
' secret-custody is a third voice in it.
title No privileged sidecar — how dind was retired, and what went with it

component "**dind sidecar** — what retires\n""--privileged"", sibling daemon, agent-reachable" as DIND <<error>>
component "internal/dind\nfive gates, a prompt, a scope scan" as GATE <<app>>
node "harnesses that must move" as MOVE <<host>> {
  component "opencode\n""docker: dind"" -> ""docker: sbx""" as OC <<app>>
  component "cecli\n""docker: dind"" -> ""docker: sbx""" as CE <<app>>
}
component "sbx sandbox\na daemon per sandbox, behind a policy" as SBX <<app>>

folder "what this costs, and to whom" as COST <<host>> {
  component "review tier\nneeds proveo's MITM; sbx has none" as REV <<error>>
  component "flow-level analytics\nflows.ndjson -> sbx policy log" as OBS <<error>>
  component "claude-in-chrome\ngreyed on sbx (SbxWhy) since 2026-10-16" as CHR <<error>>
}

GATE ARROW_MAIN DIND
OC ARROW_MAIN SBX
CE ARROW_MAIN SBX
DIND ARROW_ERROR SBX : deleted, not replaced
SBX ARROW_ERROR REV
SBX ARROW_ERROR OBS
SBX ARROW_ERROR CHR : no working path today

note bottom of DIND
  **It is privileged, and it is reachable.** A
  daemon the agent can call is a way to start a
  container proveo did not write the argv for —
  the sidecar's own warning says it may escape to
  the host. sbx gives a daemon behind a boundary.
end note

note top of GATE
  **Five gates say what it costs to keep.**
  Manifest, tier, credentials, a Dockerfile in
  scope, and an explicit yes — a lattice that
  exists only because the thing behind it is
  dangerous. Retiring it deletes the lattice too.
end note

note bottom of GATE
  **It is allowed ONLY on open + forward.** That
  is the one posture where proveo has already
  stopped enforcing anything, so dind is a daemon
  handed out exactly where there is no boundary
  left for it to breach. sbx inverts that.
end note

note bottom of REV
  **Review is the real loss.** It needs the MITM's
  CONNECT hook and a pty to ask on; sbx runs
  neither, so the tier has no implementation
  there. It is already refused on sbx today;
  this makes that refusal permanent.
end note

note top of REV
  **And it stops being proveo's to give.** An
  ask-before-connect flow inside a sandbox is the
  sandbox vendor's feature. If sbx offers one it
  will be on sbx's terms and its subscription,
  which is a product decision, not a code one.
end note

note bottom of OBS
  **The analytics survive, at lower fidelity.**
  proveo already captures ""sbx policy log"" at
  teardown and lists it as a run artifact, so
  "after the sandbox closes" is solved. What
  changes is what a record contains.
end note

note top of OBS
  **Decisions and COUNTS, not requests.** Docker
  documents host, matching rule, proxy type and
  request count, with ""--limit"" and ""--type"".
  Enough for "what did it reach, how often" —
  never "what it sent".
end note

note top of OBS
  **And the proxy sees more than it logs.**
  Measured in-sandbox: ""github.com"" and
  ""api.anthropic.com"" present the Sandboxes
  Proxy CA, so those are TERMINATED — the request
  detail exists, it is just not exposed.
end note

note bottom of OBS
  **Loopback is outside all of it.**
  ""no_proxy"" holds localhost · 127.0.0.1 · ::1,
  and sandbox-internal loopback never leaves the
  netns anyway. No proxy placement can report it,
  so that gap is structural, not a setting.
end note

note bottom of CHR
  **The network was never the cost; the token
  is.** host.docker.internal reaches the host's
  loopback from a sandbox, but the bridge needs a
  real Claude token and sbx holds a placeholder.
  So the add-on is greyed on every run now.
end note

note bottom of SBX
  **One backend, or two? ONE (2026-10-16).**
  Docker as the ""PROVEO_SBX=0"" fallback kept
  review and the flow record alive. Deleting it
  was the second decision; it is taken, and
  ""PROVEO_SBX"" is an unread variable now.
end note

note top of SBX
  **It closed secret custody (d).** On sbx the
  agent never reads a host credential file —
  sbx's proxy writes it inside the sandbox with
  placeholders. The docker path that needed a
  token on disk is deleted.
end note

legend bottom
  **The order to do it in — each step is reversible until the last**
  ==
  **1. Move the manifests.** ""opencode"" and ""cecli"" to ""docker: sbx"". Behaviour changes
  for those two harnesses only, and PROVEO_SBX=0 still reaches the old path.
  --
  **2. Stop offering it.** ""docker (dind)"" leaves the execution row; the row becomes the
  single compulsory fact it already is on the sbx harnesses. ""PROVEO_DIND"" warned and no-opped
  (warning dropped 2026-10-17).
  --
  **3. Delete the lattice.** ""internal/dind"" and its five gates, the interactive prompt,
  the scope scan, ""ScopeHasDockerfiles"", and the dind arm of ""gateAddons"".
  --
  **4. Delete the sidecar plumbing.** The link args, ""DOCKER_HOST"", the readiness wait,
  and the ""--privileged"" run — the last privileged container proveo starts.
  ==
  **Left open on purpose — ANSWERED 2026-10-16.** mitmproxy and Squid followed the
  sidecar out; review was retired rather than kept alive on a backend of its own.
  --
  **SKETCHED, then declined.** Per-request fidelity was reachable by chaining proveo's
  inspector into sbx's own proxy (the ONLY route out: ""HTTP_PROXY"" ·
  ""gateway.docker.internal:3128"") via the Kit's ""environment.variables"". Costs: proveo's
  CA trusted inside the sandbox, chain order around sbx's credential injection, and the
  ""NO_PROXY""-as-regex hazard. The Executor chose no flow record: ""sbx policy log"",
  saved at teardown, is the record. ""PROVEO_EGRESS_UPSTREAM"" no longer exists (MEASURED).
end legend
@enduml
